[{:seon.task/name "no-head-of-line-blocking"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the api writes an agent's [agent :in] with Flow's blocking send (64 slots plus a 1 MB socket, `seon.agent-proc`). Reproduced: one busy agent plus one chatty agent stalled root's whole api about 25 s while health said :running. Correct: the api never blocks on one agent (the agent proc holds overflow in its state and admits what fits); health reports progress (the oldest unanswered request), not liveness."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-busy-agent-never-stalls-another}}
 {:seon.task/name "start-takes-a-config-layer"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: a design change that needs the AGENTS section 3 gate answered first (which existing mechanism carries a per-root layer, what v1 did); Optimization gathers the evidence for the owner Sean. What exists: `seon.system/start!` takes `:max-running`, `:provider` and `:stall-ms` as options beside the config, and `seon.config/with-provider` folds a provider into the config written on main's root entity; bin/check (`:max-running 2 :stall-ms 1000`) and seon.e2e pass them. The written config is what the reconciler reads (main's root fact), so nothing bypasses main; outside the one resolver are the process's own reads: `seon.config/value` reads the process-wide `seon.config/config` (loaded once with its --set layers), so two roots in one process (the checks') cannot differ through it. Correct: a root's config in force is a layer the resolver reads (start! takes `{:set {k v}}`, resolved like a --set but per root; every reader in root's procs resolves through the root's config, not the process's); the options and with-provider go. A design change: before building, root's gate (AGENTS §3) asks which existing mechanism carries a per-root layer and what v1 did (a config per system)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "roots-log-is-bounded"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `bin/system start` truncates runs/root.log at each start (bin/system: `: > \"$LOG\"`), so the log no longer grows across starts, and the previous start's log is lost with it; within one root's life nothing bounds it. Correct: a root's log is bounded within its life by a config key's bound (bytes, its unit and provenance in resources/seon/config/default.edn) and keeps one previous log (root.log.1) across a start, so a crash's log survives the next start. Rotation happens at an event (a start, the bound reached by a write), never on a timer."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-start-past-the-bound-keeps-one-previous-log}}
 {:seon.task/name "operator-commands"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `bin/system` has status, start, stop and next; one root per checkout on its own ports (`bin/ports`); its output goes to runs/root.log. v1's bin/seon also had open (the dashboard in the browser), logs (the root's log) and reset --force (the running root unlinks its main and forks a fresh one, keeping every cache; ~/src/seon/script/seon/operator.clj). Correct: `bin/system open` opens the checkout's dashboard URL; `bin/system logs` follows runs/root.log; `bin/system reset` restarts the root on a new main of the same store (`:seon.config.root/main`), the old main kept as history and nothing deleted (deleting a record is the owner's act). Each a line in bin/system's usage and docs/machine.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/reset-starts-a-new-main-and-keeps-the-old}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-prunes-old-generations-and-check-logs"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: nothing prunes ``target/check/`` (each check run's logs) or a root's old generations (under `:seon.config.source/generations-dir`, each a worktree and a binary of about 200 MB, made by `seon.fx/build` with bin/worktree --at); deleting them is manual, keeping the running generation and the one before (docs/machine.md#disk-stays-bounded). Correct: root's merge, after the :build effect answers, removes every generation but the running one and the one before (bin/worktree --remove-at), and removes check logs past a config key's count of runs; a store holding a kept run is never removed (runs are evidence; reclaiming them is the owner's act). Deletes the manual step from docs/machine.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-build-leaves-the-running-generation-and-the-one-before}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-compute-executor-is-declared"
  :seon.task/plan "Jolt's Flow runs :compute steps on `clojure.core.async.impl.dispatch`'s compute-executor, an unbounded newCachedThreadPool of unnamed daemon threads with a 60 s keep-alive, and bounds each step with .get compute-timeout-ms 5000: a step past 5 s throws into root's errors while its worker keeps running, and the next step takes another worker. No proc in src declares :workload :compute today (`seon.render.proc/render-proc` is :mixed), so the hypothesis that it explains root's unattributed threads (lifecycle 25 -> 29 over cycles, scale 100 root threads 38 -> 55) is unproven. Correct: first prove or refute it (name the threads, or count compute workers before and after each cycle). If it holds, root passes create-flow a declared :compute-exec (a fixed pool of named threads, its size a seon.config key) and a step's bound becomes an observed overrun (its duration recorded), not an abandoned future. Estimate: root's thread count after a cycle equals the declared sum, exactly."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares
                      seon.render-test/a-slow-render-step-is-recorded-not-abandoned}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-stall-is-past-the-requests-own-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `:seon.config.agent/stall-ms` (30000, a guessed tolerance, now a config key; it was a var) is the silence after which a busy agent is observed stalled once; `seon.system/start!` takes :stall-ms, and the api's step schedules its stall check with `step/put-after!` at half of it (seon.system, :stall-check). A 25 s compute shows nothing while a legitimate long proof run reads as stalled. Correct: a request carries its declared bound (the form's, :seon.config.agent/form-ticks; a test run's is its tick budget, `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks`) and the agent proc observes a stall when that bound passes with no frame: one recorded :seon.agent/stalled naming the bound, no second number; the ticks come from the one :clock proc. Deletes stall-ms, the :stall-ms option and the :stall-check timer. The flusher's sleep this task also named is gone (durability is continuous group commit: docs/storage.md#durability-is-continuous-group-commit), and its proof with it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-stall-is-observed-past-the-requests-own-bound}}
 {:seon.task/name "event-loss-is-counted"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's buffers are literals: `seon.system/start!`'s events-in is `(a/sliding-buffer 8192)`, `seon.system/events` taps a sliding 1024 that is never untapped, and the render proc's tap is a sliding 4096 (seon.render.proc). A sliding buffer drops the oldest with no count, so the dashboard can miss events and nothing says so. Correct (the step-fn law: buffers bounded by their loss semantics): every buffer is declared in resources/seon/system.edn :chan-opts with its loss; a sliding buffer counts its drops into the api's view (:events-dropped by tap), shown on the dashboard; c/events taps are released with their reader. Estimate: one counter per drop."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-dropped-event-is-counted
                      seon.procs-test/root-buffers-come-from-the-declaration}}
 {:seon.task/name "warm-exercises-the-render-and-request-path"
  :seon.task/plan "From the branch review of jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-25, root's scratchpad branch-review.md, follow-ups): `seon.system/warm!` (private; run once at boot before the fork server forks) runs d/q, d/pull, d/db-with and d/with on a value of its own, `seon.sock/frame`, `seon.print/print-value`, and a namespace's defn and deftest through clojure.test, but no render pass (`seon.render/walker`; the render proc's comp-entry and its component cache were deleted in 7e999e41bf) and not the conversation's request path (`seon.turn/context-block`), so a forked process's first render and first turn are cold. Correct: warm! runs each once on a seed value before the fork server forks. Proof: a forked agent's first turn costs what its second does (ticks), measured."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/forked-agent-s-first-turn-costs-same-ticks}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-wait-on-main-ends-when-nothing-can-make-it-true"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md; the owner: platform stability first). seon.system/await-db parks on a promise that only a later commit satisfying pred delivers; its callers add a defect after t as a second end (seon_done, src/seon/server.clj), and test/seon/await.clj's await-quiet rides on it. When root is quiet (no message in flight, every proc parked: the reconciler's :seon.system/quiescent report) and pred does not hold, nothing inside root will ever make it hold, and the wait hangs its caller, the caller's socket and, in a test, the shard (instability 2: 1h45m at 0% CPU). Root stopping does not end it either. Correct, at the producer: root keeps its open waits (await-db's watch keys) and answers each, at the quiescence report that finds pred false, with a typed error {:seon.error/type :seon.system/never-true :t t :quiet-t q} (a fact, never a clock); and at stop! with :seon.system/stopped. A caller that waits on the outside world (a file change, a peer's commit) says so and is not answered at quiet (an explicit :outside? flag, the one exception). seon_done then answers a judge that never finished as that error. judge! puts with >!! on a 64-slot channel and parks forever on a dead, full consumer: the same rule, a put to a proc that died answers its error (see seon.system/a-put-to-a-dead-proc-is-an-error). Proofs: an await-db whose pred cannot hold on a quiet root answers :seon.system/never-true; one waiting at stop! answers :seon.system/stopped; seon_done on a run whose judge's agent died answers the error, never parks. Checked 2026-11-06 at a root on this tree. Built for a named wait (seon.system/await-db with a description; proved by seon.procs-test/a-wait-on-main-is-answered-at-the-reconcilers-quiet, green): (seon.system/await-db sys \"never\" (fn [db] false)) answered {:seon.error/type :seon.system/quiet-before-true :t .. :waits-on \"never\"} with no commit, not :seon.system/never-true as written above, and a stopped root answers :seon.system/no-agent, not :seon.system/stopped. Not built: the unnamed arity is ended only by a commit; there is no :outside? flag; seon.system/judge! still puts with a/>!! and returns nil (task a-put-to-a-dead-proc-is-an-error). No proof is named on this task. seon_done on a run whose judge agent died was not run. Merged from seon.system/await-db-has-one-arity (2026-11-10 triage): same cause (its plan says do together); that task gains one arity with no flag, ending when every proc's quiet fact says nothing can still commit, and the proof that a named wait during an embedding does not answer quiet-before-true"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/await-db-pred-cannot-hold-quiet-root-answers}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-put-to-a-dead-proc-is-an-error"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md; the owner: platform stability first). >!! onto a bounded channel whose consumer died parks the putter forever once the buffer is full: seon.system/judge! (src/seon/system.clj, 64 slots), src/seon/web.clj toward the web proc, src/seon/fork_server.clj's event reader into ev-ch. A Flow proc that dies leaves its in-channels open. Correct: a proc's death closes its in-channels (Flow's own stop closes them; a step that threw is the defect path), so a put returns false and the putter records the dead proc as its answer (a dependency is a channel a proc parks on, its death is a fact: docs/flow.md#the-step-fn-contract-every-proc-follows). Proof: a proc stopped with a full in-channel; a >!! to it returns false and its caller answers an error naming the proc. Checked 2026-11-06 by reading: seon.system/judge! (src/seon/system.clj), the web proc (src/seon/web.clj, five a/>!! calls) and the fork server (src/seon/fork_server.clj, two, into ev-ch) still call a/>!! and drop its result; judge! returns nil. No test stops a proc with a full in-channel. Not run against a dead proc."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/proc-stopped-full-channel-returns-false-caller-answers}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-named-wait-ends-when-a-runs-work-throws"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the agent loop's worktree session: with seon.turn/begin! writing a session's run on its branch without its task (fixed in jolt/agent-loop 89dca5cf3), a session's second run threw in seon.turn/work! (seon.ctx.status/task-data's contract, its task nil), and both stage tests' named waits (seon.system/await-db sys \"the implement parked\" ...; agent_test a-requests-implement-continues-its-designs-request and a-waiting-agent-parks-and-a-message-wakes-the-same-process) waited six hours: root's problems held the eval error, yet the named wait, which must end at the reconciler's quiet with :seon.system/quiet-before-true, never ended. Reproduction: revert 89dca5cf3's change to begin! and run bin/test seon.agent-test. Cause not yet found: a work! answered with :ex goes on to :judge and :branch-diff (seon.reconcile :worked), so either one of those requests is never answered on a run whose work! threw, or the run's end never reaches quiet. Correct: the run ends (its :worked-error as its reason) and the wait answers quiet-before-true; a test that waits on a fact the run cannot make goes red, never hangs. Checked 2026-11-06: seon.reconcile now ends a flight whose seon.turn/work! threw, with the reason \"seon.turn/work! threw: <message>\" and the error on the task (src/seon/reconcile.clj, the :worked-error flight key); the unit proof is the last testing block of seon.reconcile-test/a-death-names-its-cause. The proof named below is not written: no deftest of that name is in test/. The hang itself (revert 89dca5cf3, run bin/test seon.agent-test) was not re-run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-run-whose-work-throws-ends-and-the-wait-answers}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner: we may have merge conflicts or agents failing to merge, so make sure the tools to fix them by hand on root's side work as expected. What exists: the playbook is written, docs/merging.md#root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl, one entry for each of four failures with its working forms (staged and repaired once on a worktree root, 2026-10-27, with seon.fixtures/scenarios :hairy, :conflict and :semantic). Root has no conflict-resolving function of its own: my.branch/unmerged, resolve! and merge-main! run in an agent's process, so root's path is to attach to the held merge task. Of the gaps that staging found: built, (1) a worktree root commits on its own jolt/seon-merges-<worktree> (seon.config reads #seon/own-branch), (2) my.root/write-back!, (6) my.root/hold!, proved by seon.reconcile-test/root-takes-a-running-task-back-and-releases-it-again; filed as their own tasks, 3, 4 and 7 as seon.system/a-behind-merge-ends-in-a-fact, 5 found not to occur (an attach writes no :seon.task/model, docs/merging.md), 9 as root-repair-reads-are-cheap. Left here: gap 8 (my.root/cancel! leaves the task's problem entries open, and the page counts closed entries), not checked against the code as it is now; reproduce it first on a worktree root (never runs/store), fix it at its cause, and add what the playbook gains to its section."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/my-root-cancel-task-leaves-none-problem-entries}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-behind-merge-ends-in-a-fact"
  :seon.task/plan "Gaps 3, 4 and 7 of root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl (staged 2026-10-27, to reproduce first: stage fixtures :conflict, attach the merge task, merge main and resolve, then give main a new namespace with seon.source/text-tx). (a) A merge stays :behind forever after main gains a namespace: the form of a namespace new on main reaches the branch with :ns nil, so main differs at that key on every merge (`seon.merge/program-of` :ns read, merge.clj, or the carry in `seon.merge/merge-tx`, merge.clj); control: a change to an existing namespace merges on the second run. Fix at the carry; check a namespace made from an agent's REPL (the :newns scenario) the same way. (b) Nothing bounds :behind reruns: a :behind run ends green so max-merge-turns never counts it (reconcile.clj ends the run :behind and the task stays ready); under a model that loops forever and root causes (quiescence only) never names it. A :behind of a branch that already merged main at the head it was refused at is a defect fact, and the task is held with that evidence. (c) A behind merge branch is judged before main is merged: an attached agent's REPL runs main's program while its branch db holds the old base, so the proofs pass on the branch and the held reason reads proof-unknown. seon_done (or the judge) refuses a branch whose base is behind with the writer's :seon.merge/behind message and its fix before judging proofs, or attach to a merge task runs my.branch/merge-main! as the turn loop does. Proof to write: merge-main! followed by the octopus merge is not :behind when main gained a namespace; a :behind rerun at an unchanged head holds the task. Checked 2026-11-06: (a) is built and proved, in the direction a branch makes a namespace and main takes it, by seon.merge-test/a-merge-carries-the-namespace-of-a-form-it-carries (green); main making the namespace and the branch merging main (the scenario above) has no test. (b) is built as a count, not as unchanged-head: :seon.config.merge/max-behind (3) judgings of one merge task that end :behind hold it for root with the writer evidence (seon.reconcile-test/a-merge-behind-again-and-again-is-held-for-root, green); a rerun at an unchanged head is not held earlier. (c) is built for the explanation only: a behind branch is told \"behind N\" before proof-unknown (seon.reconcile-test/a-branch-behind-main-is-told-so-first, green); whether attach runs merge-main! was not checked. Tests and default.edn cite these as seon.system/a-behind-merge-is-bounded-and-merges-main-first, a name no task has."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/merge-main-followed-octopus-merge-behind-main-gained}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-repair-reads-are-cheap"
  :seon.task/plan "Gaps 9 and the smaller ones of root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl: root's repair reads cost and say too little. (1) `my.root/problems` took 66 ms / 100 MB to 1,063 ms / 1.7 GB allocated on a store of about 25 tasks and `seon.server/task-summaries` about 560 ms / 850-915 MB a call. (2) The page names root causes only at quiescence, so a run that never ends shows 'a31 idle' and nothing links a cause to its run or task; the conflicts' content is not on the page. (3) A held merge appears twice in `my.root/problems` (:merge and :failed), and the footer counts closed entries. (4) After a successful write-back retry the task keeps its stale :seon.task/commit-error (retract it); a namespace with no file records a commit-error naming each key twice (reconcile.clj, unfiled-error). (5) A request with raw newlines answers one :seon.server/defect per line: a line that does not read as a map answers once, naming the rule one request per line, newlines escaped. Proof to write: contracted calls and bytes of one my.root/problems read on 25 tasks, flat in the conversation (a cost proof); one answer for a multi-line request. Checked 2026-11-06 at a root of 295 tasks with no runs: (1) my.root/problems 147 ms and 755 KB, seon.server/task-summaries 63 ms and 103 MB, far under the numbers above; the input that made them slow (tasks with run histories) was not staged. (5) is still so: a request of two lines sent to the socket answered two :seon.server/defect values, the second a contract error of seon.server/answer. (2), (3) and (4) were not checked."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/request-two-lines-sent-socket-answers-error-naming}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-eval-never-moves-main"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-30 (a scratch in-agent test listing main's transactions around each eval, on 3348c59cee): every eval of any agent writes main two or three times: seon.system/record-agents writes the agent's facts (seon.task/agent-tx) whenever its view entry's observed-keys change, and the view's :running (the form an eval runs, set at its start and retracted at its end) and :status change at every eval. Of those transactions most carry no datom of their own (only :seon.tx/branch and :seon.tx/runtime): agent-tx rewrites every member, unchanged values transact to nothing, and seon.writer/commit-batch still commits each, so main's commit moves for nothing. Cost: every agent hears main moved after any agent's eval (docs/merging.md#main-moved-is-said-at-every-eval) and its page's branch views, keyed by main's commit (seon.repl/render-ctx), redraw (15.5M ticks against 9.2M a read, measured 2026-10-29), which is work in agents squared; seon.server-test/a-later-read-draws-no-page-cold-and-costs-what-the-one-before-did is red because no two of its nine reads see one main. The form running is already the eval's record on the agent's own branch, so main's copy is a second producer of one view. Still so: ran 2026-11-06 at a scripted root, main's :max-tx moved by 8 across one agent eval of `(+ 1 1)` where a root read moves it by 1. Proposed (needs the owner's go: it changes what main records of a live agent): (1) the writer commits no transaction whose datoms are all no-ops (one commit fewer, nothing lost); (2) :running and per-eval :status are root's live view (the dashboard renders root's view, as it does other live state), never main facts; main keeps an agent's spawn, end, death and stall, which change what other agents and the judge read. The page already re-runs only on what each component read (seon.render/render!, d/reads-touched?; the context session, 2026-10-30), so the 15.5M ticks a move costs is what the move sets off in every agent (hearing main moved, merging it into its branch, re-framing its walk). (1) alone leaves main moving every eval, by :running's real set and retract, so the proof stays red without (2); (1) is a change to our Datahike fork's datahike.writer/transact-batch! (an all-no-op batch answers its reports with no commit) or to seon.writer/commit-batch, a dependency change either way, which also waits for the owner's go. Ran 2026-11-06 at a root on this tree: main's :max-tx was 536871206 before one eval of (+ 1 1) in an attached agent and 536871214 after (+8); a root read moves it by 1. The transactions around it carry :seon.process/running and :seon.agent/status datoms (history shows two datoms of :seon.agent/status and one of :seon.process/running in each). The proof named below is green, and would stay green with that bug: it compares costs only of pairs of reads that saw the same main (its \"same\" filter, asserting only that some pair exists), so it does not fail when main moves at every read. The input that shows the bug is the :max-tx of main across one agent eval."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-later-read-draws-no-page-cold-and-costs-what-the-one-before-did}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-that-dies-starting-answers-its-attach"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-01 (jolt/search-index-inherited, a contract error in seon.agent/schema-moved during begin-run!): an agent that threw in its start (seon.agent/init!) was logged by root (\"a forked child threw out of its code: clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo seon.agent/schema-moved invalid-input\") and the test's attach (seon.agent-test/in-agent, through seon.server's attach) waited forever: the fork server idle in its run loop (its fiber dump), no agent process, the test's shard at 0% CPU for 10 minutes until it was killed. Every wait ends with its value or a typed error (docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs); the producer that owed the answer is the agent's start, and its death is a fact root holds. Correct: a forked agent's throw before its hello is the spawn's answer (seon.system's answer-spawn already answers a death before the hello: find why this one is not taken, a death by throw in init! against one by exit), so the attach answers the error. Reproduce: an agent whose init! throws (a test environment whose begin-run! throws), and its attach answering :dead or the error within the test's run, never a stall. Checked 2026-11-06 by reading: seon.system/end-agent answers the spawn of an agent that ends before its hello (answer-spawn, with the death, src/seon/system.clj); an agent whose init! throws was not staged, so whether this attach still hangs is not known. No test is named."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/attach-agent-init-throws-answers-dead-error-within}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-running-root-spawns-the-binary-it-was-built-as"
  :seon.task/plan "Also seen 2026-11-05: a root built before 36498f1806 spawned its vector helper while the main tree was on a branch without :seon.config.web/utc-offset; the helper read that checkout config, was refused, and left five :seon.vector/helper-died entries that stay after the next root starts. Seen 2026-11-05 in the standing store's problems before its erase, 32 entries of 5 kinds: target/seon ended before its model loaded (exit 1), the config does not satisfy :seon.config/config, with keys missing or unknown (:seon.config.store/max-value-chars, :seon.config.render/similar, :seon.config.native/sqlite-lib). Cause not confirmed, the likely one: a root spawns its model child by the path target/seon, and building another branch in that tree replaces the binary under it, so the child and the root's config disagree. Correct: a process a root spawns is the root's own build (its binary held open, or named by generation), so no rebuild reaches it; seon.check-test/a-stale-binary-is-refused is this class's neighbour. Proof to write: replacing the file at the path leaves a running root's next child its own build. Checked 2026-11-06 by reading: seon.vector/start! (src/seon/vector.clj) still spawns its helper by the path (cfg/checkout)/target/seon; nothing names or holds a generation for it. Not run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/replacing-file-target-seon-leaves-running-root-s}}
 {:seon.task/name "shutdown-and-death-are-dataflow"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: root's stop parks on the drain and an agent's end on its socket's close, with no bound (`seon.system/stop!`, `seon.agent-proc/stop!`); a child's exit is an event (`seon.fork-server`'s :exits channel, jolt.io-poller's exit-chan). What is left is one clock, which docs/clocks.md#time-decides-owed-its-owners-fix names here: `seon.system/start!` passes Flow's :stop-timeout-ms, :seon.config.root/flow-stop-ms, what flow/stop waits for a proc's exit; a proc still running is named in stop!'s :graph, printed on ROOT STOPPED. Correct: a proc that does not exit is a recorded problem with no time deciding. Whether Flow can stop with no bound is not checked; it is Flow's mechanism (docs/flow.md). Deletes :seon.config.root/flow-stop-ms. No proof is written for it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/proc-does-exit-stop-recorded-problem-naming-time}} 
 {:seon.task/name "the-system-entity-and-root-as-an-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: stage 3 needs stages 1a and 1b of docs/users.md landed, and a store erase with Web and Context warned; the managing session sequences it. Owner, 2026-11-09: the system's entity is :seon.graph/* (\"sure\"); \"root I think is mostly the go between for flow communication and isn't doing anything that can allow an agent or the user to take down the system\"; \"The user doesn't need to be an agent under root\". Stage 3 of docs/users.md#the-stages-agreed-2026-11-09, needing stages 1a and 1b. Optimization owns the store and boot; Web owns seon.system's root-session code (seon.system/root-agent-tx, which writes its pattern once as its role's name and one join; the special message path). What exists: main's root entity {:seon.system/id :root} holds the system's facts (boot, config, quiescence, source) beside root's own session; root's session is a thing apart; :seon.agent/root marks root's agents. Correct: the system's facts on one entity of :seon.graph/* attributes, read by root's procs, which no agent renders; root is the agent keyed \"root\", an ordinary agent working its task, made with the user's link to it; root's patterns lose their aliases; :seon.agent/root goes, liveness read from :seon.agent/boot. Stored keywords are renamed by erasing the standing store, never a migration (docs/decisions.md), with Web and Context warned. Proofs, red first: a boot writes the system's facts on :seon.graph/* and nothing on a :seon.system/id entity; root answers as the agent keyed \"root\" through the same requests as any agent; no attribute :seon.agent/root in the schema or the store."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-task-files-kind-is-dropped-at-load"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: the owner's design ruling about kinds (a thing is a map; choosing is merge) comes first (below). What exists: every task file declares a kind (306 declarations write :seon.task/kind :work), and seon.system/task-files makes each declaration a :whole task through seon.template/request, dropping the written kind (\"the kind is derived here and never written in a file\"). So every task on a store reads :whole (331 of 331 on a store seeded from main 2026-11-10), and the code that decides by kind for :work, :design or :implement is never reached from a task file: seon.problems/uncited-tasks judged only :implement and so never fired (fixed on jolt/task-cites); seon.render.ui hides a status line unless :work or :whole; seon.ctx.status and seon.reconcile read :plan and :plan-before only for :design; seon.task/merges? treats :whole as merging itself.

What allowed it: the kind is written in the files and decided in the loader, two places for one fact, and the written one is read by nothing; the readers key on a kind the data no longer carries.

The change waits on the owner's design ruling about kinds (a thing is a map; choosing is merge), which the managing session brings after the restart: either the files stop writing a kind (306 lines go) and every reader of :work, :design and :implement is retired with it, or the kind becomes data the loader keeps. Not to be fixed piecemeal. Evidence for the ruling: this task, and the list of readers that decide by kind kept in the Context session's state.

Proofs, once ruled: a task file's declaration loads with the kind the ruling says, and no reader keys on a kind no task on the store holds."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-session-is-a-branch-and-bin-prove-asks-root"
  :seon.task/plan "The design these steps serve, 2026-11-11: one test service keyed by (test, digest) item for every asker (seon.prove/the-test-service-runs-each-item-once-for-every-asker; notes test-system-as-a-flow-2026-11-10.md). Owner's rulings, 2026-11-10 (docs/decisions.md): a worktree session's changed files are written as a branch of the standing store, deleted when it lands or is dropped, and root proves that branch as it proves an agent's; bin/prove and bin/land are thin wrappers. Test plan step 4. What exists: seon.system/changed-tx (the landing bridge's landed-tx and Optimization's boot catch-up use it on main; Optimization owns it: this calls it with a branch, never a second importer); seon.prove, the service an agent's prove asks. Plan: bin/prove sends the files that differ from the tree main was last brought to (git diff since the source fact's :tree) to root; root writes them with changed-tx on a branch session-<worktree> and proves it with the same service and verdict cache, answering the run's answer map; bin/land asks the same and merges on green. A dropped session's branch is deleted by bin/worktree --remove; one whose worktree is gone is a problem row until deleted. Removes in the same landing: test_runner's plan-of, tests-of, program-db, the kept program store per git tree and reach-graph-of (about 300 lines), bin/prove's planning and marker. Experiment first: changed-tx onto a fresh branch for a one-file change; an agent spawned there resolves the new definition and not a deleted one. Proofs to write: a-tree-on-a-branch-costs-its-changed-files (bytes and calls grow with 1 -> 10 changed files, flat across programs of 10 and 100 forms); a-session-and-an-agent-at-one-digest-run-it-once."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-root-that-took-a-landing-stops"
  :seon.task/plan "First read (Optimization, 2026-11-10, notes optimization-rootstop-hang-2026-11-10.md): the hung root's sample shows a thread looping in Chez's foreign entry (dlsym, failing on some images) and others waiting on allocation; batch 12b added no native symbol, so the reading is code loaded live re-binding a foreign entry on each call; seon.program/a-merge-loaded-in-place-unbinds-what-it-deletes (live code reaching deleted definitions after a bridge) is the likeliest sibling: read both together. bin/system stop may end a root far past its measured stop (owner's yes, 2026-11-10), using Tests' bin/evidence and seon.system/stall-state, recording why. Seen 2026-11-10 at 18:47 on the standing root (main 20cf1174c0's binary, up 39 minutes, so past its fork server's warm-up): `bin/land` bridged a landing into its main (the Datahike pin 32ff1c10 and seon.contract's private-checked switch: main 8a68b49995), `bin/update` built and ran `bin/system stop`, and the stop waited 13 minutes. Root 97309 held about 220% CPU with no child process left; a 3 s sample had nearly every thread in a condition wait and a few in dyld's symbol lookup; its log's last line was ROOT LISTENING 7888. A TERM did not end it; the managing session ended it with KILL after capturing, and `bin/update` then started the new root. Not the warm-up window (a stop in a fork server's first ~35 s waits its warm-up: measured 31.7 s on a root 2 s old, 27 ms on one up 60 s; fixed by ending the boot fork server's group at a stop on branch jolt/faststop, not on main when this was written). Two defects: (1) what `seon.system/stop!` waits on that a bridged landing can leave unfinished (a proc that does not exit at Flow's stop, the writer mid-batch, a reader in a loop: the stop's phases timed on a root that has just taken `seon.system/landed-tx` will say which; a load in place of a changed dependency pin is the first thing to try); (2) a root that does not end on TERM: docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs holds for a stop too, so `bin/system stop` says after a bound of its own measured time what it waited on, and ends the root's process group. What should it cost: a stop is a stop fact (about 20 ms), a close and a join of procs that have nothing to finish: well under a second at any uptime. Reproduce: start a root on a store, land a change that moves a pin through `bin/land --bridge`, stop it. Wiki: docs/root.md, docs/merging.md#a-branch-lands-with-binland."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-root-that-took-a-landing-stops-in-a-second}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-root-code-edit-makes-the-binary-not-current"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by Optimization (2026-11-11): after src/my/root.clj and src/seon/system.clj changed, ```seon.system/binary-current``` answered current (\"current by reach\"), so a root ran a binary that did not hold the code its main held. What exists: binary-current reads git's changed paths between the binary's tree and main's; a src file is not current only when a var one of its forms defines is in the root-reach of db's reach graph (seon.program/root-reach), and code the binary runs only by name (system.edn's procs, the server's ops, an agent's REPL helpers that my.root holds) is not a reference, so an edit to it reads as agent-side and current. Cause to confirm first: which var of the two files root-reach left out, and why its roots (```seon.program/binary-program```) did not name it. Correct: every var the binary runs is in root-reach because the data naming it is a root (the names in system.edn, the ops, the REPL helpers), never a list of files; then the sweep: every src file whose vars root runs by name. Proof: on a tree whose only change is a form of seon.system, or of my.root, binary-current answers not current, naming the var. docs/source.md#the-binary-records-its-program states it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-root-code-edit-makes-the-binary-not-current}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-top-bar-names-the-checkout-the-system-runs-in"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen (Web, 2026-11-11, the readable worktree's root on a store cloned from the ladder worktree): every page's top bar (seon.render.ui/source-line, the html render of :seon.system/source) read \"ladder · detached · a15778238d\": the path of the checkout the store was cloned from, no branch, and this tree's commit; its hover: /Users/sean/src/seon-next/.worktrees/ladder commit a15778238d... A person reading the bar is told the wrong system. What exists: :seon.system/source is written by an import (seon.system/import-of, src/seon/system.clj:1061; written by the seed and by my.root/import!), whose fact carries {:root :branch :commit :tree} from the read; when main already holds the read's digest it answers {:digest} alone and nothing is written (import-of's (= digest (:digest held)) case; my.root/import! answers held), so a store whose program equals this tree's keeps the :root and :branch another checkout wrote. How this one came to hold this tree's commit with the other path and no branch is not yet shown: reproduce first (clone a store into a second worktree whose tree has the same program, start its root, pull :seon.system/source). The class: which checkout a system runs in is a fact of the boot, not of the program's digest; candidates: the boot writes :root :branch :commit on its own fact (seon.system/booted-source) and the bar reads that, or the digest-equal case still writes the checkout keys. The standing system shows the same class by design (Point, 2026-11-11): its source is the merges tree (config/local.edn :seon.config.source/root, .worktrees/merges on jolt/merges, where agent merges write back), so its bar read \"merges · detached · bd202d9f17\", a commit not on git main; a person should read what it runs, main plus the merged tasks not yet in git (\"main, plus 1 merged task not yet in git\"), read from the source fact and the branch. Proof: seon.system-test/a-cloned-store-names-the-checkout-it-boots-in (a store seeded from one checkout booted in another names the second's folder and branch)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-cloned-store-names-the-checkout-it-boots-in}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-landing-keeps-the-agents-that-are-working"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. Found 2026-11-11 by Data model on the standing system: batch 77's landing restarted root (bin/update), and every live agent was lost one turn in; the outside agent learned it from a refused eval (\"works no task now ... seon_attach works one again\"). With a landing every 15 minutes no agent on the standing system outlives one. What exists: bin/update stops and starts root on main's new binary; an agent is a child of root's fork server and ends with root (docs/machine.md: a root's processes end with it); the reap marks running work :lost, model agents' work is spawned again from main, outside agents' waits for an attach (seon.reconcile/a-lost-outside-task-waits-visibly-for-its-agent). Correct, the owner's direction for running a program from the db (2026-11-11, \"load all the code in the system in the right order in one eval\"): a landing whose binary need not change (no pin, no native, no runtime file) brings the RUNNING root to main's program in place (seon.program/load-diff! from the program it runs to main's, as an agent's spawn does), its agents untouched; only a landing that must change the binary restarts, and that restart says so to each agent it ends, before it ends it. Proof: a landing of a src-only change on a running root keeps a live agent working, and the root then runs main's new form. First measure what load-diff! of a typical landing costs on the standing root (Tests' whole load was 134 s for every form; a landing changes a few)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-src-landing-keeps-a-live-agent-working}}
 {:seon.task/name "binary-current-costs-what-the-change-reaches"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. Measured 2026-11-11 on the standing system (read-only): seon.system/binary-current over 11 landing tree pairs cost 12.5 s and 17.6 GB allocated, about 1.1 s and 1.6 GB a call. It builds the reach graph of the whole db each call (its docstring: one git diff, one reach graph of db, O(changed files' forms)), so the question \"does this landing reach root-run code\" costs the whole program. It will be asked at every landing (the no-restart landing) and is already asked at every spawn refusal and stale-binary row. Correct: the reach graph is kept with the program it describes (a value keyed by the program's digest, as seon.program/graph-index's cache is), so a call costs the diff and the changed forms' lookups. Bound in a cost proof: binary-current of a one-file change costs under a fixed number of ticks whatever the program's size."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-cost-test/binary-current-costs-the-change-not-the-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-start-that-sweeps-transacts-on-a-live-connection"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. Seen 2026-11-11 (Context's worktree ladder, /tmp/ladder-root-spin.log and -spin2.log, both starts on an EXISTING store): seon.system/start!'s own transact (the boot's catch-up, root.clj ~1546) dispatched on a nil writer: \"No implementation of method: :-dispatch! of protocol datahike.writer/PWriter found for class: nil\", i.e. a transact on a RELEASED connection (Deep fixes: release sets the state to :released; Datahike 66b5e71c, with the next Jolt pin, raises :connection-has-been-released naming the op). Suspect (code read, not shown): the start's sweep path, root.clj ~1531: when retain! swept snapshots, start! releases (:conn opened), runs seon.db/compact-if-due!, which opens and then CLOSES the LMDB environment of the same path (klmdb/close-env!) and renames the data file, then opens the store again. If any connection to that path is still referenced then (drop-trees! or retain! connecting a branch without releasing it, or Datahike's per-[store branch] count left above zero), the close and the rename happen under it and the reconnect is handed a released one. Settle first: a red-first test that starts root twice on one store with :seon.config.store/keep-commits small enough that the second start sweeps, then transacts; and the count of connects and releases of that path across one start. Correct, at the cause: the compaction runs before any connection to the store is made in a start (its own docstring says \"before it opens the store, as the store's only process\"), never between a release and a reopen."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-start-that-sweeps-transacts-on-a-live-connection}} {:seon.task/name "a-stall-that-waits-on-the-system-is-the-systems"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-12 (Context, worktree pairclean on main 624b1dd00e, evidence in the notes folder's hang-pairclean-0320): the system ran at 100% CPU for 16 minutes while its agent sat at 0% CPU blocked in read, its unix socket to the system open (agent fd 19 to root fd 28). The agent's last act was model call #8 and 10 answered evals; no call #9, no outbound connection. The system's stall check (seon.agent-proc's :stall?: a request in flight, no frame for :stall-ms, no effect outstanding by :fx-out) flagged the AGENT and took its evidence (stalls/: the agent's sample, lsof, ps). Nothing of the system's own state was taken, though the system was the stuck side. What allowed it: :fx-out counts only the effects the agent asked over its pipe. An agent's asks over its socket to the system's server (the ops a turn sends) are not counted, so an agent waiting on the system reads as an agent that went quiet. Correct: what the system owes an agent on any channel is counted where the stall is judged (the server's open requests from that agent, by its key, beside :fx-out). A quiet agent that the system owes an answer is the system's stall: its evidence is the system's (seon.system/stall-state, a fiber dump, a sample of the system's process) and the problem names the op it waits on. Proof, red first: an agent with an open server request to the system and no frame past the bound produces a stall naming the system and the op, never :seon.agent/stalled. See docs/failure-points.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-proc-test/a-quiet-agent-the-system-owes-is-the-systems-stall}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-api-that-throws-on-every-message-spins-on-its-own-errors"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by reading (runtime, 2026-11-13, step-contract-hang): root's Flow error channel is piped into the api's :back, and the api's :error arm handles its own throws too (::lost). 0d1718d446 stopped an error answer being answered again through its :dest; but an api whose step throws on every message, its :error messages included, re-takes each of its own errors: each throw makes one :error, the next step throws on it, one message spinning for good (the 11-12 spin's shape, without the :dest). The cause to fix, in ```seon.system/answer-taken``` and the api's :error arm (docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs): an error the api raised about itself, taken back, is answered once and never handled by the step that threw (its own :error is recorded through a path that cannot throw, or dropped as already recorded). Estimate: one check per :error message. Proof, red first: an api step that throws on every message, one error taken back, the api quiet after it (no second :error)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/an-api-that-throws-on-every-message-records-it-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-stop-or-crash-resumes-from-one-resume-point-and-says-what-it-lost"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner B10: prepared for power loss and a hard crash, and a proper shutdown that loses nothing and resumes from a proper resume point; data loss on power loss accepted, errors shown, a path to resume always. Upgrades are online first (a new runtime from the new pin starts beside the old on the same data and takes over); when that cannot work, proper shutdown, rebuild, migration, clean start. What exists: the store opens MDB_NOSYNC|SAFENOSYNC and root's flusher syncs after each commit seen (137 flushes for 164 commits [M]); after an OS crash our LMDB fork's first writable opener restores to the newest steady mark (mdb.c:7130-7150, 3546-3603), so only a non-first opener gets MDB_UNSETTLED; stop! writes :seon.system/stopped durably and the next boot's previous-end (system.clj:1576) reads it or finds :unclean; agents end with root; boot-wakes wakes waiting agents. Today a clean stop and a crash lose the same in-flight evals, long asks and effect children. Design: :seon.system/resume on main's root entity, {boot, t, agents [key branch task-id outside?], asks [uuid]}. A clean stop quiesces (stop admitting turns, spawns, effects; each in-flight eval, ask and effect finishes within its own bound, never a shutdown clock; one past its bound ends typed), writes resume point and stop fact in one flushed transaction, then stop!. A boot after a clean end respawns each model agent on its branch, marks outside agents :lost until their session attaches, answers open asks. A boot after an unclean end derives the same value (live agents with no ended-ms, open asks, one AVET seek each), records cause :unclean and a problems row :unclean-end naming ended agents, reopened asks and unanswered evals; after power loss it also reports from deps' mdb_env_restore the rolled-back txn ids (from N to M). A write-back never gets ahead of the store. If the boot cannot read the store, erase and reseed from main's files. Replaces stop!'s interrupt-all, Flow's :stop-timeout-ms and :seon.config.root/flow-stop-ms, boot-wakes alone, and corrects failure-points' MDB_UNSETTLED row. Also: root's load-changes! should call rearm! for schema-only changes, as an agent's register! does [M: contract followed a live schema change, 32.5M ticks]. Deps owes the restore ids, a gate test with a changed boot id, and eval through the AOT cache. Power loss itself is never simulated."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/an-unclean-boot-derives-the-resume-point seon.system-test/a-clean-stop-waits-for-work-by-its-own-bound seon.system-test/a-killed-root-loses-nothing-committed seon.system-test/a-clean-stop-writes-a-resume-point-and-the-boot-resumes-it seon.system-test/an-unclean-end-is-shown}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer"}}

 {:seon.task/name "a-stop-that-never-answers-ends-with-its-evidence"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists [M]: bin/system stop sends TERM to the root's holder and waits in bin/held --end (bin/held:38-52) for its exit status, a select with no deadline. A root that never exits holds the stop forever: context's shard root, 2026-11-14, held its bin/test 10 minutes, two threads looping in a failing dlsym (S_foreign_entry -> lookup), the main thread parked, TERM doing nothing. [H] Jolt's zlib-procs (host/chez/java/zlib.ss:173-181) binds under a mutex and never remembers a failed bind; against it [M], zlib binds in a fresh jolt; the next hang's Scheme-level dump settles it (deps' if so). The design (plans/runtime-stop-bound.md): the stop is an effect with a declared deadline, the one clock principles allow. :seon.config.root/stop-ms, its default from n >= 10 measured clean stops (ROOT STOPPED's :ms, which no log keeps today); bin/held --end SIG PID FIFO N [MS] prints `overran MS` and exits 4 past it; bin/system stop reads the key through the binary's one resolver, captures bin/evidence into runs/stop-overran-<pid>/, kills the group by the existing kill path, and answers STOP OVERRAN :seon.system/stop-overran; bin/update's restart and bin/test's teardown read that line. Shared machinery: run end to end through bin/system stop, bin/update, bin/test and bin/check. Needs root's yes on the plan."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-stop-that-never-answers-ends-with-its-evidence}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
